From 9fd1bbba715d759b5c87e1872f05cb7844da1b38 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 23:34:13 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 12/13] FEXInterpreter, FileManager: keep FEX's own descriptors
 out of the guest's fd range

FEX opens descriptors before the guest starts: the FEXServer socket, the log
descriptor, and FileManager's rootfs and /proc directory descriptors. They
took the lowest free numbers, so a guest's first open() returned 4 or more
instead of 3. Programs, and build outputs that record descriptor numbers,
see the difference from Linux.

New FEX::MoveFDOutOfGuestRange (Common/FDUtils.h) moves such a descriptor
just below the RLIMIT_NOFILE soft limit, keeping its close-on-exec flag, and
closes the original. It leaves the descriptor where it is when the limit is
under 256. Applied to the FEXServer connection, the server-provided log
descriptor, the stderr and file log descriptors, and FileManager's rootfs
and /proc directories.

Evidence, 64K host, FEX_HOSTPAGEMODE=force, a static x86-64 test whose first
call is open("/dev/null"):
  before: first open fd=4
  after:  first open fd=3 (Linux: 3)
Ubuntu 24.04 bash from the RootFS still reads files and lists /proc/self/fd.

Found in POWERarm, where an Arch Linux ARM program under a rootfs got 4 (the
socket at 3, rootfs at 6, /proc at 7).
